sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/phyllotaxis-attractor-classification--grok-4-7--1> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Interval-certify cylindrical Fibonacci and Lucas branches" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/phyllotaxis-attractor-classification> ;
    dct:description "Implement the discrete cylindrical map exactly as defined by Atela, Golé and Hotton 2003, whose fixed points are cylindrical lattices and whose bifurcation parameter is the growth parameter of their diagram. Do not substitute a different potential. On a uniform grid of that parameter across the interval their paper analyzes, certify attracting cycles by interval Newton on the return map: a cycle is certified when an interval box maps into itself and the interval derivative excludes magnitude 1 or greater. Classify certified limits by parastichy pair: consecutive Fibonacci numbers, consecutive Lucas numbers, or the bijugate doubles. A trajectory whose interval enclosure stays away from every classified cycle is an unclassified attractor. The golden-angle branch is the divergence 2π/φ^2 with φ = (1 + sqrt(5))/2. Full Lebesgue measure of its basin is not claimed by a grid; the grid certifies which cycles exist." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A certified partition of the paper's parameter interval into basins of Fibonacci, Lucas and bijugate cycles, with every grid point accounted for." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: An interval-certified attractor that is none of those families. That is the falsifier the problem states: an attractor outside the classified set." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/computation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Encode the Atela–Golé–Hotton cylindrical map from the paper, not a proxy. 2. Sweep their growth parameter on a stated grid. 3. Certify attracting cycles by interval Newton. 4. Label each cycle as Fibonacci, Lucas, bijugate, or unclassified. 5. Emit the certified partition of the parameter interval." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): hard" ;
    skos:note "Tool: interval-arithmetic" ;
    skos:note "Tool: dynamical-systems" ;
    skos:note "Tool: simulation" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch14-01> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch14-05> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch14-06> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch14-09> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch14-02> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1007/s00332-002-0513-1> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.68.2098> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/phyllotaxis-attractor-classification> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Rigorous classification of attractors in dynamical phyllotaxis" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1007/s00332-002-0513-1> dct:title "A Dynamical System for Plant Pattern Formation: A Rigorous Analysis" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Atela, Golé and Hotton 2003, A dynamical system for plant pattern formation" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.68.2098> dct:title "Phyllotaxis as a physical self-organized growth process" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Douady and Couder 1992, the physical model this map discretizes" .
}

